s.  This construction works for every \\(k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, while the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   is attainable.  Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the set stated in the boxed answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\text{If a sunny line contains three points of }P,\n\\text{ then two of those points have either equal }x,\n\\text{ equal }y\\text{ or equal }x+y.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in P\\) be collinear on a sunny line.\nIf none of the equalities \\(a_i=a_j,\\;b_i=b_j,\\;a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\) holds,\nthe three points determine a unique line whose direction vector is\n\\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\).  Because the three points are distinct,\nthe vector is non\u2011zero and its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\nConsequently the line is parallel to none of \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\),\ncontradicting the hypothesis that the line is sunny.  \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line can contain at most two points of }P\n\\text{ that are not already covered by a non\u2011sunny line.}}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines.  By (3) the sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of\n\\(P\\) that are not covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.  Each non\u2011sunny line\nbelongs to one of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) defined\nabove, and any such line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, and all the others contain fewer).\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\)\nnon\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n\\,(n-k)\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(P\\) must be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn\\,(n-k)+2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nc=k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\n\\]\n\ntake the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-(k+2)+1=n-k\n\\]\n\nsuch lines.  The union of the \\(L_c\\)\u2019s contains every point of \\(P\\) whose\ncoordinates satisfy \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nQ=\\{(a,b)\\in P\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(Q\\) is the right\u2011angled lattice triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(k+1,1),(1,k+1)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|Q|=\\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}(s-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-(i-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nThe points of \\(Q\\) that lie on \\(S_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i+1,2),\\dots ,\\;(k+1-i,i),\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly \\(k+1-i\\) points.  The families \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) are\npairwise distinct, and\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}=|Q|.\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) cover all points of \\(Q\\).\n\n**(iii) Verification.**  \nAltogether we have used  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny anti\u2011diagonal lines \\(L_c\\);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) of slope \\(1\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and\nevery point of \\(P\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines (points with\n\\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) are on some \\(L_c\\); points with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) are on some\n\\(S_i\\)).  Thus a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists\nfor this \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4), all\nthose values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration can have more than\n\\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\nThe explicit construction above shows that for each integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines that covers every point of \\(P\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]"
