\n\nwhich is non‑sunny and covers all points of \\(P\\) whose sum equals \\(n+1\\).\n\nFor the remaining points use the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k-1\\) lines are also non‑sunny.  \nTogether with \\(D\\) they give exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nAll points of \\(P\\) with \\(x\\le n-k-1\\) lie on some \\(V_i\\); all points with\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered\n\nThe points not yet covered are those with \\(x\\ge n-k\\):\n\\[\nQ=\\{(a,b)\\in P\\mid a\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have\n\\(b\\le k+1-r\\).  Hence \\(Q\\) is the lattice right‑angled triangle\n\n\\[\n\\{(n-k+r,\\,s)\\mid r\\ge1,\\;s\\ge1,\\;r+s\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\npoints.\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(Q\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-(n-k+i-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny (it is not\nparallel to \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\)).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in Q\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge n-k\\); solving\n\\(b=a-(n-k+i-1)\\) gives the points of \\(Q\\) that lie on \\(S_i\\):\n\\[\n(n-k+i,1),\\;(n-k+i+1,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(n,\\,k+1-i\\bigr).\n\\]\nThus \\(S_i\\) contains exactly \\(k+1-i\\) points of \\(Q\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) are pairwise distinct, and by (3)\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|Q|.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) cover all points of \\(Q\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Verification of the whole configuration\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{the diagonal }D)\n\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{vertical lines }V_i)\n\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines }S_i)\n\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(S_i\\)’s).  \nEvery point of \\(P\\) belongs either to \\(D\\), to some \\(V_i\\), or t