es (the diagonal plus \\(n-3\\) other\n   non‑sunny lines).  \n\n   Counting the lines we would then have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{sunny}}+\\underbrace{(n-2)}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\\ge n+0,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. **all** \\(n\\) lines are already used just to cover the points of \\(D\\).\n   No line would be left to cover the points of \\(P\\setminus D\\) (those with\n   \\(a+b\\le n\\)), which are non‑empty for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  This contradiction\n   shows that a configuration with two or more sunny lines cannot exist; hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Realising \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All of them are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(P\\).  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Realising \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) together with the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{v}:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{h}:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These three lines cover all points of \\(P\\) except possibly those with\n   \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  The remaining points are exactly the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in P\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n,\\;a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a smaller copy of the original triangle.  It is covered by the\n   single sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{s}:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, \\(\\ell_{s}\\) contains the points \\((2,2),(3,3),\\dots ,(m,m)\\) where\n   \\(m=\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\); the points that are not on \\(\\ell_{s}\\) are\n   already on \\(\\ell_{v}\\) or \\(\\ell_{h}\\).  Consequently the four lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{d},\\;\\ell_{v},\\;\\ell_{h},\\;\\ell_{s}\n   \\]\n\n   (with \\(\\ell_{s}\\) the only sunny one) cover all points of \\(P\\).  Since we\n   need exactly \\(n\\) lines, we simply add any \\(n-4\\) further non‑sunny lines\n   (for instance the remain