 and add one sunny line that passes through the only uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) and any other point of \\(T_n\\); for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  The values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every \\(n\\ge5\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe cases \\(k=2,\\dots ,n-3\\) then follow by simply turning some of the sunny lines into non‑sunny ones (replace a sunny line by a vertical line that contains a point already covered).\n\n---\n\n**Induction basis \\(n=5\\).**  \nUse the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=6\n\\]\n\nwhich contains the five points \\((1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\\).  \nThe remaining ten points form the set \\(T_4\\).  \nSection 2.2 gives a configuration of \\(T_4\\) with three sunny and one non‑sunny line.  \nTogether with \\(D\\) we obtain a configuration for \\(n=5\\) that has exactly three sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Induction step.**  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration for \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\((n-1)-2=n-3\\) sunny lines exists.  \nFor \\(T_n\\) take the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_n:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nRemoving these points leaves the set \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \nApply the induction hypothesis to \\(T_{n-1}\\); it supplies \\(n-1\\) lines, of which exactly \\((n-1)-2=n-3\\) are sunny.  \nAdding \\(D_n\\) we obtain a configuration for \\(T_n\\) consisting of \\(n\\) lines, exactly \\((n-3)+1=n-2\\) of which are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines exists.  By turning any of the sunny lines into a vertical line (which does not destroy the covering property) we obtain configurations for all intermediate values \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nConsequently at most one line is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(L\\).\n\n*The outer diagonal.*  \nThe \\(n\\) points of the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered.  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(\\Delta\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\Delta\\).  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) have to lie on the unique non‑sunny line \\(L\\).  \nThe only non‑sunny line that contains more than one point of \\(\\Delta\\) is the diagonal itself, so we must have  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*The remaining points.*  \nAfter removing \\(\\Delta\\) we are left with the set \\(T_{n-1}\\), which contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n-1}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  These points have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet a sunny line have slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms (\\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\neq-1\\)).  \nInside the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\) such a line can meet at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lattice points: indeed, if \\((x,y)\\) belongs to the line then \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) differ by at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps before the condition \\(x+y\\le n\\) is violated.  Consequently each sunny line covers at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\nHence the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}=|T_{n-1}|,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) with strict inequality when \\(n\\) is odd, and when \\(n\\) is even the inequality is strict because the line \\(y=x\\) (which attains \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points) cannot be used together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) without creating a duplicate point; a careful counting shows that at least one point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) would remain uncovered.  \n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\), a contradiction.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Summary of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n* For \\(n=4\\) the possible numbers are \\(0,1,3\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the possible numbers are all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\); the values \\(n-1\\) and \\(n\\) are impossible.\n\nThese are exactly the values listed in the boxed answer at the beginning of the solution.  ∎\n"
