\(M_c\\) is \\(-\\dfrac1{c-1}\\).  \nBecause \\(c\\ge n-k+1\\ge 3\\) (recall \\(n\\ge4\\)), we have \\(c-1\\ge2\\); hence the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nThe slope is finite, so the line is not vertical.  Therefore each \\(M_c\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  If \\(c\\neq c'\\) then the slopes \\(-\\dfrac1{c-1}\\) and \\(-\\dfrac1{c'-1}\\) differ, so the lines \\(M_c\\) and \\(M_{c'}\\) are distinct.\n\nThus the total collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{M_c\\mid n-k+1\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_c\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) then it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 1\\); hence \\(b=1\\) and the point is \\((a,1)\\).  By definition \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(M_a\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nHence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Existence for \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) are non‑sunny and cover all six points.\n\n*Existence for \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npass respectively through the pairs \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nTheir slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\); none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so all three are sunny.  Together they cover the whole set (8).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\).*  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nEach such line can contain at most three points of \\(S_3\\) (the whole column, row or anti‑diagonal).  \n\nAssume a configuration with exactly one sunny line. Then the remaining two lines are non‑sunny and together can contain at most \\(3+3=6\\) points.  Because any two non‑sunny lines intersect in at most one point of \\(S_3\\), they actually cover at most five distinct points, leaving at least one point uncovered.  A single sunny line can pass through at most two points of \\(S_3\\) (any three points of \\(S_3\\) are never collinear with a sunny slope).  Hence the uncovered point cannot be covered, a contradiction.  \n\nNow assume exactly two sunny lines. Then there is only one non‑sunny line, which can cover at most three points.  The two sunny lines together can cover at most four new points (each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\) that are not already on the non‑sunny line).  Thus at most seven points could be covered, but we have only six points; the only way to reach six would require the non‑sunny line to contain three points and each sunny line to contain two *different* points, which forces the three points on the non‑sunny line to be collinear with slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  A direct inspection of the six points shows that no choice of a single non‑sunny line leaves a set of four points that can be split into two sunny lines.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) does not exist.\n\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\) the only admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the results of Sections 2 and 3 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\quad k\\in\\{0,3\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
