\\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-2i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n* the denominator \\(n+1-i>0\\);\n* the numerator \\(n+1-2i\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) would give slope \\(0\\));\n* solving \\(\\frac{n+1-2i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}=-1\\) gives \\(2n+2-i=0\\), impossible for positive \\(n\\).\n\nHence the slope is never \\(0\\), never undefined and never \\(-1\\); each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then the slopes \\(\\frac{n+1-2i}{n+1-i}\\) and \\(\\frac{n+1-2j}{n+1-j}\\) differ, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)=P_a\\in L_a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge2\\) write \\(b=n+1-2i\\) for the unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\); then \\(a=n+1-i\\) and \\((a,b)=Q_i\\in L_i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  \nConsequently \\(k=n\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(n\\ge4\\): any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge4\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\)* the constructions of Section 1 apply.\n\nAssume \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine the following family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Vertical lines*  \n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \nV_j:\\;x=j\\quad (j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThere are \\(n-k\\) such lines; all are non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le c\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nM_c:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{c-1}\\,x+\\frac{c}{c-1},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nthe line through the two points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).\n\n**Verification of the properties of the \\(M_c\\).**\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(M_c\\) is \\(-\\dfrac1{c-1}\\).  \nBecause \\(c\\ge n-k+1\\ge 3\\) (recall \\(n\\ge4\\)), we have \\(c-1\\ge2\\); hence the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nThe slope is finite, so the line is not vertical.  Therefore each \\(M_c\\) is sunny.\n\n*