n    the vertical choice).\n\n5.  After fixing the vertical lines (5) the uncovered part of the triangle is  \n\n    \\[\n    T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n    \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n    \\tag{6}\n    \\]\n\n    The right‑most column of \\(T_k\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\);\n    the left‑most column contains the point \\((n-k+1,k)\\).\n\n6.  Suppose \\(k\\ge 2\\) and \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \n    The point \\((n-k+1,k)\\) is not on a vertical line, so it must be covered\n    by a sunny line.  \n    The point \\((n,1)\\) is also not on a vertical line, hence it must be covered\n    by a sunny line as well.  \n    Because a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, the **same**\n    sunny line has to contain both \\((n-k+1,k)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n    Its slope is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1-k}{\\,n-(n-k+1)}=\\frac{1-k}{k-1}=-1,\n    \\]\n\n    which is forbidden for a sunny line – a contradiction.  \n    Consequently no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists when\n    \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n7.  For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the only possible values are therefore \\(k=0\\) and\n    \\(k=1\\).  Both are realizable:\n\n    * \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\);\n    * \\(k=1\\): the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with\n      the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\)).\n\n8.  The case \\(n=3\\) must be examined separately.  \n    A direct check shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible (the same slope \\(-1\\)\n    argument applied to the points \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)).  \n    The three sunny lines  \n\n    \\[\n    y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n    \\]\n\n    cover all six points of \\(S_3\\); together with the constructions for\n    \\(k=0,1\\) we obtain the admissible set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\nThus the list of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated\nabove. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) define the *\\(a\\)-th column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\},\\qquad |C_a|=h_a=n+1-a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the column heights are \\(h_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Properties of sunny lines  \n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line meets each column, each row and each diagonal\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) in at most one lattice point; otherwise two points of the\nline would have the same abscissa, ordinate or sum, forcing the slope to be\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The hypotenuse  \n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nlie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the hypotenuse of the triangle).  \nTwo distinct points of \\(H_n\\) have the same sum, therefore a sunny line can\ncontain **at most one** point of \\(H_n\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  The \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n\\(H_n\\); consequently the other \\(n-k\\) points of \\(H_n\\) must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Form of the non‑sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nOnly the vertical and horizontal lines intersect the hypotenuse; a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) meets the hypotenuse **only** when it coincides with it\n(\\(c=n+1\\)).  If we used the whole hypotenuse as a non‑sunny line, it would\nalready contain all \\(n\\) points of \\(H_n\\), leaving only \\(n-1\\) lines for the\nremaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points of \\(S_n\\setminus H_n\\); this is impossible\nbecause each of the remaining lines can meet a column in at most one point,\nso at most \\((n-1)n\\) points could be covered, which is strictly smaller than\n\\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the hypotenuse itself cannot be used.\n\nTherefore each of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines must be **vertical** or **horizontal**\nand each of them contains exactly one point of the hypotenuse.  By symmetry we\nmay assume that they are the vertical lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(If they were horizontal, the same argument applied to rows would give an\nidentical conclusion.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The uncovered region  \n\nAfter fixing the vertical lines (3) the points of \\(S_n\\) that are still\nuncovered form\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigcup_{a=n-k+1}^{n} C_a\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA short count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑most column of \\(T_k\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\); the\nleft‑most column contains the point \\((n-k+1,k)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nThe point \\((n-k+1,k)\\) does **not** lie on any of the vertical lines (3), so it\nmust be covered by a sunny line.  \nThe point \\((n,1)\\) also does not lie on a vertical line, hence it must be\ncovered by a sunny line as well.\n\nA sunny line can meet each column in at most one point; consequently the **same**\nsunny line has to contain both \\((n-k+1,k)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  Its slope is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-k}{\\,n-(n-k+1)}=\\frac{1-k}{k-1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden for a sunny line.  This contradiction shows that no\nconfiguration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The admissible values for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nFrom the previous paragraph we obtain that for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable:\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all\n  non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).\n  The only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny.\n  Hence exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),\\;(2,1),(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible: the two points\n\\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), so they cannot be\ncovered by two distinct sunny lines together with the remaining points.\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\nL_{2}:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the pairs \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) and\n\\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\); together they cover all six points, and all three lines\nare sunny.  Together with the constructions for \\(k=0\\) (the three vertical\nlines) and \\(k=1\\) (two vertical lines and the sunny line through \\((3,1)\\)\nand \\((1,2)\\)) we obtain the admissible set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results with the explicit constructions we have\ncompletely determined the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\