fying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line has slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(|p|,|q|\\ge 1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1),( -1,1)\\).  \n   The vector \\((q,p)\\) is the minimal integer step along the line; therefore any two\n   lattice points on the line differ by a multiple of \\((q,p)\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Column argument.**  \n   For a fixed abscissa \\(x=a\\) the set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points,\n   i.e. the *\\(a\\)-th column* has height \\(h_a=n+1-a\\) (\\(h_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1\\)).  \n\n   *A sunny line meets each column in at most one point.*  \n   Hence, if we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) points of any column can be\n   covered by them; the remaining points of that column must be covered by a\n   non‑sunny line (which can be taken vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n4. **Necessity of many vertical lines.**  \n   Let the number of sunny lines be \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \n   All columns whose height exceeds \\(k\\) need a vertical line, because a sunny\n   line can contribute at most \\(k\\) points to such a column.  \n   The columns with height \\(>k\\) are precisely the first \\(n-k\\) columns\n   (\\(a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\)).  \n   Therefore we need at least \\(n-k\\) vertical (hence non‑sunny) lines.\n   Since we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, **all of them must be vertical**\n   and they must be the lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).\n\n5. **The remaining columns.**  \n   After the vertical lines are fixed, the uncovered part of \\(S_n\\) consists of the\n   last \\(k\\) columns:\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n   The number of points in \\(T_k\\) is\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet each of these \\(k\\) columns in at most\n   one point, so the total number of points that the sunny lines can possibly\n   cover inside \\(T_k\\) is at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\).  \n   Because \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\) for every \\(k\\ge1\\), the counting\n   obstruction does **not** rule out any \\(k\\ge1\\); a more delicate geometric\n   obstruction is needed.\n\n6. **Geometric obstruction for \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Consider the right‑most column \\(a=n\\); it contains the single point\n   \\((n,1)\\).  \n   In the set \\(T_k\\) the left‑most column is \\(a=n-k+1\\) and it contains the\n   point \\((n-k+1,k)\\) (the highest point of that column).  \n   Because there is no vertical line for these columns, the point\n   \\((n-k+1,k)\\) must be covered by a sunny line.  \n   Any sunny line that passes through \\((n-k+1,k)\\) meets the column \\(a=n\\) at\n   exactly one point; the only possible point is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Hence the same sunny line must contain both \\((n-k+1,k)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\n   The slope of the line through these two points is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{k-1}{(n-k+1)-n}= \\frac{k-1}{-(k-1)}=-1 .\n   \\]\n   Slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden for a sunny line – it is a non‑sunny line.\n   Consequently **no sunny line can cover the point \\((n-k+1,k)\\)** when\n   \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n   Therefore for any \\(k\\ge 2\\) the configuration described in step 4 cannot be\n   completed, i.e. a set of \\(n\\) lines with at least two sunny lines does not\n   exist.\n\n7. **Consequences.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the argument of step 6 fails because the “left‑most” column of\n     \\(T_k\\) coincides with the right‑most column when \\(k=2\\); a separate check\n     shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, while \\(k=3\\) is attainable (see\n     §8).\n\n8. **Explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and\n   cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).\n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is sunny.\n   Thus we obtain exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\n   L_{2}:y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\n   L_{3}:y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover the six points of \\(S_{3}\\); all three lines are sunny, giving\n   \\(k=3\\).\n\n   (The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\) follows from the same slope‑\\(-1\\)\n   argument applied to the unique column of height \\(2\\).)\n\n9. **Final answer** is the boxed statement in the Verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on the number of lattice points of a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms,\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\), \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\).\nIf two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\) lie on the line then  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1)=\\frac pq\\,(x_2-x_1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(p\\mid (y_2-y_1)\\) and \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).\nThus any two lattice points of the line differ by a multiple of the vector\n\\((q,p)\\); consequently all lattice points of the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) we have \\(|x-x_0|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|y-y_0|\\le n-1\\);\nhence \\(|t|\\le\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\).\nTherefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n1+ \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  (The inequality is strict because \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\) for a\nsunny line.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Columns of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\nh_a=n+1-a\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints; we call \\(h_a\\) the *height* of column \\(a\\).\nThus the heights are \\(h_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1\\).\n\nA sunny line meets each column in **at most one** point, because two points of\nthe same column would have the same abscissa and would differ by a vector\n\\((0,\\Delta b)\\), contradicting (2) unless the direction vector is \\((0,1)\\),\ni.e. the line is vertical (non‑sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of vertical lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\)