ine through these two points is\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{k-1}{(n-k+1)-n}\n   =\\frac{k-1}{-(k-1)}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nSlope \\(-1\\) is prohibited for a sunny line, a contradiction.\nHence **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) has six points.\nThe argument of §5 does not apply because the “left‑most’’ column of \\(T_k\\)\ncoincides with the right‑most one when \\(k=2\\).\nA direct check shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, whereas \\(k=3\\) is\nrealised by the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Existence for the admissible values\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is sunny.\n  Hence exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).  \n  The three lines displayed in (7) are all sunny and together contain the six\n  points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus every value listed in the Verdict is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the impossibility results of §§5–6 with the constructions of §7 we\nobtain the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assump