            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on the number of lattice points of a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms,\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\), \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\).\nIf two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\) lie on the line then  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1)=\\frac pq\\,(x_2-x_1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(p\\mid (y_2-y_1)\\) and \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).\nThus any two lattice points of the line differ by a multiple of the vector\n\\((q,p)\\); consequently all lattice points of the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) we have \\(|x-x_0|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|y-y_0|\\le n-1\\);\nhence \\(|t|\\le\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\).\nTherefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n1+ \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  (The inequality is strict because \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\) for a\nsunny line.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Columns of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\nh_a=n+1-a\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints; we call \\(h_a\\) the *height* of column \\(a\\).\nThus the heights are \\(h_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1\\).\n\nA sunny line meets each column in **at most one** point, because two points of\nthe same column would have the same abscissa and would differ by a vector\n\\((0,\\Delta b)\\), contradicting (2) unless the direction vector is \\((0,1)\\),\ni.e. the line is vertical (non‑sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of vertical lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny; they may be taken vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider a column whose height exceeds \\(k\\).  \nSince each sunny line can contribute at most one point to that column,\nthe column would still contain uncovered points unless a non‑sunny line passes\nthrough it.  Consequently **every column with height \\(>k\\) must contain a\nvertical line**.\n\nFrom (4) the columns with height \\(>k\\) are precisely\n\\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\); there are \\(n-k\\) of them.\nHence we need at least \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.\nBecause we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, **all of them are forced to be\nthe vertical lines**\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The remaining region\n\nAfter fixing the vertical lines (5) the uncovered part of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigcup_{a=n-k+1}^{n} C_a\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality follows from (4):\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe region \\(T_k\\) consists of the last \\(k\\) columns, whose heights are\n\\(k,k-1,\\dots ,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nIn \\(T_k\\) the **right‑most column** is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\); the **left‑most**\ncolumn is \\(C_{\\,n-k+1}\\) and its highest point is \\((n-k+1,k)\\).\n\nBecause there is no vertical line for these columns, the point\n\\((n-k+1,k)\\) must be covered by a sunny line.\nAny line that contains \\((n-k+1,k)\\) meets the column \\(x=n\\) in at most one\npoint, and the only point of that column is \\((n,1)\\); therefore the same\nsunny line would have to contain both \\((n-k+1,k)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe slope of the line through these two points is\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{k-1}{(n-k+1)-n}\n   =\\frac{k-1}{-(k-1)}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nSlope \\(-1\\) is prohibited for a sunny line, a contradiction.\nHence **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) has six points.\nThe argument of §5 does not apply because the “left‑most’’ column of \\(T_k\\)\ncoincides with the right‑most one when \\(k=2\\).\nA direct check shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, whereas \\(k=3\\) is\nrealised by the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Existence for the admissible values\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is sunny.\n  Hence exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).  \n  The three lines displayed in (7) are all sunny and together contain the six\n  points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus every value listed in the Verdict is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the impossibility results of §§5–6 with the constructions of §7 we\nobtain the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
