se a sunny line is not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\),\n\\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\); therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Columns of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(S_n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=h_a=n+1-a .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the heights of the columns are  \n\n\\[\nh_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets each column in **at most one** point, because two points\nwith the same abscissa would differ by a vector \\((0,\\Delta b)\\), contradicting\n(2) unless the line were vertical (non‑sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\nBoth horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines intersect each column in **exactly one**\npoint (if the column is tall enough).  \nConsequently, if a non‑vertical non‑sunny line is replaced by a vertical line\nthrough the leftmost column that is still uncovered, the set of uncovered\npoints does not increase, and the number of sunny lines stays unchanged.\nRepeating this replacement we obtain a new covering with the same numbers\n\\(n\\) and \\(k\\) in which **all non‑sunny lines are vertical**.\n\nHence we may (and shall) assume that the non‑sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The uncovered triangle  \n\nAfter fixing the vertical lines (5) the points that are still uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the last \\(k\\) columns of the original triangle.\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet each of these \\(k\\) columns in at most\none point; therefore the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k^{2}\\) points of\n\\(T_k\\).  Since \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\) for all \\(k\\ge1\\), the\ncardinality (6) does not by itself forbid any value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).\nThe set \\(T_k\\) always contains the two right‑most columns\n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\nC_{n}   =\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three points listed in (7).  \nAny line that contains two of them has one of the forbidden slopes:\n\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\);\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) lie on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)).\n\nHence **no sunny line can contain more than one of the three points**.\nSince the three points must all be covered, at least three sunny lines are\nnecessary.  This contradicts the assumption that only \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines\nare available.  Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),\\;(2,1),(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\nL_{2}:y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare all sunny and together contain the six points; therefore \\(k=3\\) is