                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced form  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m=\\frac pq\\;(q>0,\\;(p,q)=1),\\qquad (p,q)\\neq(1,-1),(-1,1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\) lie on the line then  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1)=\\frac pq\\,(x_2-x_1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(p\\mid (y_2-y_1)\\) and \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).  \nAll lattice points of the line are therefore of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\)\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\), we have\n\\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\) unless \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) (slope \\( \\pm1\\)).\nIf \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\) then from (3) the step in the \\(x\\)–direction is at\nleast \\(2\\) (or the step in the \\(y\\)–direction is at least \\(2\\)).  \nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) we have \\(|x-x_0|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|y-y_0|\\le n-1\\);\nconsequently  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n1+2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.2  The case slope \\(1\\) (i.e. \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\))\n\nHere the vector in (3) is \\((1,1)\\).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be the point of the line with the smallest value of \\(a+b\\)\n(which is \\(\\ge2\\)).  Adding \\(t\\) steps gives the point \\((a+t,b+t)\\).\nThe condition \\(a+t+b+t\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n+1-(a+b)}{2}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\max}+1\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints, the same bound as in (4).\n\n---\n\nCombining the two sub‑cases, **every sunny line contains at most**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Columns of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\n|C_a|=h_a=n+1-a .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the heights of the columns are  \n\n\\[\nh_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets each column in **at most one** point: if two points of a\nline had the same abscissa \\(a\\) they would differ by a vector \\((0,\\Delta b)\\),\nwhich contradicts (3) unless the line were vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), a\nnon‑sunny line.  Hence the “at most one per column’’ claim is justified.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines (Exchange Lemma)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny; each of them is either\n\n* vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),\n* horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nBoth horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines intersect each column in **exactly one**\npoint (provided the column is tall enough).  Consequently, if such a line\ncovers a point \\((a,b)\\) we may replace it by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); this\nvertical line certainly covers \\((a,b)\\) and cannot cover any point that was\nalready covered by another vertical line, because the abscissae of vertical\nlines are distinct.\n\nWe perform the following algorithm:\n\n1. While there exists a non‑vertical non‑sunny line, pick the leftmost column\n   that still contains an uncovered point.\n2. Replace the chosen line by the vertical line through that column.\n\nEach replacement does **not increase** the number of uncovered points,\nbecause the new vertical line covers the point that motivated the replacement\nand possibly additional points, but never loses any that were already covered.\nThe new vertical line has a new abscissa, so distinctness of the lines is\npreserved.  The algorithm terminates after at most \\(n-k\\) steps, because each\nstep reduces the number of non‑vertical non‑sunny lines by one.\n\nHence we may (and shall) assume that the non‑sunny lines are precisely the\nvertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The uncovered triangle  \n\nAfter fixing the vertical lines (7) the points that remain uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the last \\(k\\) columns of the original triangle.  A direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet each of these \\(k\\) columns in at most\none point (Section 2), therefore the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k^{2}\\)\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Since \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\) for every \\(k\\ge1\\),\nthe cardinality (8) alone does not forbid any value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).\nThe set \\(T_k\\) always contains the two right‑most columns  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\nC_{n}   =\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three points listed in (9).  Any line that contains two of them\nhas one of the forbidden slopes:\n\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\);\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) lie on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)).\n\nHence **no sunny line can contain more than one of these three points**.\nSince the three points must all be covered, at least three sunny lines are\nnecessary.  This contradicts the assumption that only \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines\nare available.  Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),\\;(2,1),(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\nL_{2}:y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare all sunny (their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) and together contain the\nsix points; therefore \\(k=3\\) is realizable.\n\nIf we tried to use exactly two sunny lines, the three points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) would again force the same obstruction as in\nSection 5, showing that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Thus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all\n  non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is\n  sunny.  Exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* **\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines displayed in Section 6 cover all\n  six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus every value listed in (10)–(11) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results of Sections 5 and 6 with the explicit\nconstructions of Section 7 we obtain the complete description of the possible\nnumbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
