bility proved in\n   steps 6 and 7.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with reduced rational slope\n\\(m=p/q\\;(q>0,\\;(p,q)=1)\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1),(-1,1)\\).\nIf \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\) lie on the line then  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1)=\\frac pq\\,(x_2-x_1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(p\\mid (y_2-y_1)\\) and \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).\nThus all lattice points of the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) we have \\(|x-x_0|\\le n-1\\) and\n\\(|y-y_0|\\le n-1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\),\n\\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\); therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Columns of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(S_n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=h_a=n+1-a .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the heights of the columns are  \n\n\\[\nh_1=n,\\;h_2=n-1,\\dots ,h_n=1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets each column in **at most one** point, because two points\nwith the same abscissa would differ by a vector \\((0,\\Delta b)\\), contradicting\n(2) unless the line were vertical (non‑sunny).\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------