}\n\\]\n\nEach of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet each column in at most one point, therefore the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k^{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  Since \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\) for every \\(k\\ge1\\), the counting (8) does **not** forbid any value of \\(k\\); a finer geometric obstruction is needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Geometric obstruction for \\(k\\ge2\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nBecause the vertical lines (7) occupy the first \\(n-k\\) columns, the set \\(T_k\\) always contains the two right‑most columns\n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\nC_{n}   =\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three points listed in (9).  \nAny line that contains two of them has one of the forbidden slopes:\n\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\);\n* \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) lie on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)).\n\nHence **no sunny line can contain more than one of these three points**.  \nSince all three points belong to \\(T_k\\), at least three sunny lines are required to cover them.  Consequently a configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\) (because we would need at least three sunny lines but only \\(k\\) sunny lines are available).  Therefore for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),\\;(2,1),(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\nL_{2}:y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=-2x+5\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nare all sunny and together contain the six points; hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n\nA direct inspection shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible: the three points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) force the same obstruction as in step 5, leaving only one sunny line to cover three points, which cannot be done.  Thus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny\n  and cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is\n  sunny.  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* **\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines displayed in (11) cover all\n  points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nNo other values of \\(k\\) are possible by the impossibility proved in\nsteps 5 and 6.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results of Sections 5 and 6 with the explicit\nconstructions of Section 7 we obtain the complete description of the\npossible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
