                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Bound for a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line \\(L\\) in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac pq\\,x+c ,\\qquad q>0,\\;(p,q)=1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\), we have\n\\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) then  \n\n\\[\n(x_2-x_1,y_2-y_1)=t\\,(q,p)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z),\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n(x_2+y_2)-(x_1+y_1)=t(p+q).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the values of the sum \\(a+b\\) for lattice points of \\(L\\) form an\narithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) distinct values of \\(a+b\\) can occur,\nand therefore  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The special case \\(p=q=1\\), i.e. slope \\(1\\), is covered by the same\nargument because then \\(p+q=2\\).) ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Columns)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=h_a=n+1-a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot contain two points of the same column:\nif \\((a,b_1),(a,b_2)\\in L\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\), then the difference vector is\n\\((0,b_2-b_1)\\), which would force the direction vector \\((q,p)\\) of \\(L\\) to\nsatisfy \\(q=0\\), i.e. \\(L\\) would be vertical, contradicting the definition of\nsunny. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{any sunny line meets each column }C_a\\text{ in at most one point.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\nThen there exists another family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(S_n\\), also containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, such that all\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) are vertical.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) already has only vertical non‑sunny lines we are done.\nOtherwise pick a non‑sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) that is not vertical; thus\n\\(L\\) is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).\nBy Lemma 2, \\(L\\) meets each column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point.\n\nLet \\(V\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of abscissae of the vertical lines\npresent in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(n-k-1\\)\nvertical lines at this stage, \\(|V|\\le n-k-1\\).  Consequently among the first\n\\(n-k\\) columns there exists at least one column \\(C_{a_0}\\) with\n\\(a_0\\notin V\\); choose the smallest such \\(a_0\\) (the leftmost uncovered\ncolumn).\n\nReplace \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a_0\\).  This new line is distinct from\nall existing vertical lines because \\(a_0\\notin V\\).  Any point of \\(S_n\\) that\nwas covered by \\(L\\) lies in some column \\(C_a\\); if \\(a=a_0\\) the point also\nlies on the new vertical line, and if \\(a\\neq a_0\\) its covering is unchanged.\nHence the replacement does not decrease the set of covered points.\nThe number of sunny lines is unchanged because we replaced a non‑sunny line\nby another non‑sunny line.\n\nRepeating the operation for every non‑vertical non‑sunny line yields a family\n\\(\\mathcal L'\\) in which all non‑sunny lines are vertical, while the total\nnumber of lines and the number of sunny lines remain the same. ∎\n\nFrom now on we assume that the non‑sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Uncovered triangle)\n\nAfter fixing the vertical lines (7) the points of \\(S_n\\) that are still\nuncovered form  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the last \\(k\\) columns of the original triangle.\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Simple counting bound)\n\nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line can meet each of the \\(k\\) columns of \\(T_k\\) in at\nmost one point; therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot k = k^{2}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Since \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\) for every \\(k\\ge1\\),\nthe inequality (9) does **not** by itself forbid any value of \\(k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (Geometric obstruction for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\).  Then \\(T_k\\) necessarily contains the three\npoints  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of the points in (10) has slope\n\n* \\(0\\) (the horizontal line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\));\n* \\(\\infty\\) (the vertical line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\));\n* \\(-1\\) (the line through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\)).\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, so **no sunny line can\ncontain more than one of the points in (10)**.  Since the three points must be\ncovered, at least three sunny lines are required, contradicting the assumption\nthat only \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines are available.  Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 7 (The special case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\nL_{2}:y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\ncover all six points, showing that \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\nIf we tried to use exactly two sunny lines, the three points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) would again force a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\), which is impossible for a sunny line.  Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 8 (Constructions for the admissible values)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny\n  and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.\n  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* **\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines displayed in Lemma 7 cover all\n  points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThese constructions show that every value listed in (11)–(12) can indeed be\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion ###\n\nCombining the impossibility results of Lemmas 6 and 7 with the explicit\nconstructions of Lemma 8 we obtain the complete description of the possible\nnumbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If } n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If } n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
            },
